# Licence of the captured data

**Crawl permission: established. Reuse: not established.**

## Crawling

Checked 2026-09-24. `app.snowflake.com/robots.txt` is 257 bytes:

    User-agent: *
    Disallow: /
    Allow: /$
    Allow: /login
    Allow: /marketplace
    Allow: /marketplace/
    Allow: /static/
    Allow: /v0/guest/
    Allow: /pep.guest.v1.GuestService/
    Allow: /bootstrap/marketplace
    Allow: /sitemap.xml
    Sitemap: https://app.snowflake.com/sitemap.xml

The publisher **names the sitemap twice** — once as an explicit `Allow` and once
as a `Sitemap:` directive advertising it to crawlers. Evaluated with longest-match
(`wss.robots`), which is what RFC 9309 and Google specify. Note that
`urllib.robotparser` reads this file as forbidding everything, including `/`,
because it returns the first matching rule; that is a parser defect, not a
restriction the publisher wrote.

## Reuse

**Unaddressed.** No licence statement appears in the sitemap or on the marketplace
pages, and Snowflake's terms have not been read for a redistribution grant. Do not
assert one.

What this repository holds is narrower than a dataset: **a list of URLs the
publisher publishes for search engines**, plus counts derived from it. There is no
vendor content — no dataset, no description, no schema, no price — because the
listing pages contain none that is reachable.

## Personal data

None. Listings are corporate data products. The slug's leading token often looks
like a vendor name (`lseg-starmine-...`), but it is derived from a product title,
not a published field, and it identifies companies rather than individuals.
